import json,html
r=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];a=r/'frst-110-resources/equiano-vocabulary-assets';d=json.loads((a/'words.json').read_text());e=html.escape
sections=''
for category in dict.fromkeys(x['category'] for x in d):
 rows=''
 for x in [w for w in d if w['category']==category]:
  refs='';
  for o in x['occurrences']:
   refs+=f'<p class="context"><a href="{o["url"]}">Ch. {"V" if o["chapter"]==5 else "VI"} · transcription p. {o["original_page"]}</a><br><q>{e(o["phrase"])}</q></p>'
  rows+=f'<article class="word" id="{x["id"]}" data-category="{e(category)}" data-search="{e((x["term"]+" "+x["definition"]).lower())}"><h3>{e(x["term"])}</h3><div><p>{e(x["definition"])}</p><details><summary>See the word in the chapter</summary>{refs}</details></div></article>'
 sections+=f'<section class="word-group" data-group="{e(category)}"><h2>{e(category)}</h2>{rows}</section>'
moments=[('V · sold again','contrition → dejection → faint hope','He first interprets renewed enslavement as punishment for his own conduct. Religious self-blame, sorrow, and hope coexist.'),('V · leaving England','anguish · tumultuous emotions · unutterable grief','Departure closes the possibility of rescue. The language intensifies beyond ordinary disappointment.'),('V · arrival and King','horror → relief → an aching heart','Reassurances reduce immediate fear, but watching the English ship depart renews the pain of separation.'),('V · living under King','fortitude · gratitude · fear','He resolves to endure. Favorable treatment and gratitude do not remove ownership or the danger of sale.'),('V · witnessing others’ suffering','pity · melancholy · despair','Some emotions belong to other enslaved people; the narrator also describes his own response to their suffering.'),('VI · stolen trading goods','consternation · distress · indignation','Shock and suffering become anger at an injustice. Indignation names the moral judgment within the anger.'),('VI · Joseph Clipson','horror · alarm · an anxious boding mind','The kidnapping makes legal freedom appear insecure. He fears future loss of liberty, not merely present hardship.'),('VI · preparing for Philadelphia','hope · alacrity → astonishment','The prospect of buying freedom energizes work. The threat of another sale abruptly reverses his expectations.'),('VI · Farmer confirms his account','exquisite pain → fullest joy · gratitude','His emotions change rapidly when the captain supports him and King names a purchase price. This is a promise of possible freedom, not manumission yet.'),('VI · assault and care','benumbed · uneasy · weeping','Bodily numbness describes Equiano’s injuries. Uneasiness and tears describe Farmer’s response; identify whose feeling is being named.')]
feel=''.join(f'<tr><th scope="row">{e(k)}</th><td><strong>{e(v)}</strong><p>{e(t)}</p></td></tr>' for k,v,t in moments)
options=''.join(f'<option>{e(c)}</option>' for c in dict.fromkeys(x['category'] for x in d))
page=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Equiano V–VI · Vocabulary, feelings, and quiz</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="equiano-assets/equiano.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="equiano-vocabulary-assets/page.css"><script src="equiano-vocabulary-assets/quiz.js" defer></script></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to vocabulary</a><nav class="course-nav"><a href="../courses/frst-110.html">FRST 110 · Course materials</a><a href="equiano-chapters-3-4.html">Chapters III–IV</a><a href="equiano-and-omar-ibn-said.html">Equiano and Omar ibn Said</a></nav><main id="main"><header class="page-header"><h1>Equiano V–VI: vocabulary and feelings</h1><p><a href="equiano-chapters-5-6.html">Chapters 5–6 · reading guide and maps</a></p><p><a href="equiano-vocabulary-self-test.html">Practice the 37-item handout: vocabulary, occupations, and currency</a></p><p>Monday, November 2, 2026 · Volume I, Chapters V–VI</p><p>{len(d)} word senses and expressions from both complete chapters. Definitions explain the meaning in the passage. Emotional language comes first.</p></header><nav class="section-nav"><a href="#feelings">Feelings in context</a><a href="#quiz">Self-check quiz</a><a href="#vocabulary">Full vocabulary</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a></nav><section id="feelings" class="reading-section"><h2>Feelings across the two chapters</h2><p>Identify whose feeling is described, what causes it, and how it changes. Several feelings can coexist. Gratitude, resignation, and hope do not mean that enslavement becomes acceptable.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Passage</th><th>Language and context</th></tr></thead><tbody>{feel}</tbody></table><p><strong>Important distinctions:</strong> contrition concerns perceived wrongdoing; dejection concerns low spirits; despair concerns lost hope. Consternation is shocked confusion; indignation is anger at injustice. Fortitude is endurance, while alacrity is eager energy. “Indifferent” has two separate senses below.</p></section><section id="quiz" class="reading-section"><h2>Vocabulary self-check</h2><p>Choose the meaning of the named word as used in its chapter. Each round has up to 20 questions. Answers include the definition and the original context. Scores stay on this page and are not submitted.</p><div class="quiz-controls"><label for="quiz-category">Practice set</label><select id="quiz-category"><option value="all">All vocabulary</option>{options}</select><button id="start" type="button">Start a new round</button></div><p id="quiz-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Choose a set and start a round.</p><div id="question"></div><div id="answers"></div><div id="feedback" aria-live="polite"></div><button id="next" hidden type="button">Next word</button><noscript>The full vocabulary and chapter contexts below work without JavaScript. The interactive quiz needs JavaScript.</noscript></section><section id="vocabulary" class="reading-section"><h2>Full vocabulary</h2><div class="filters"><label for="search">Find a word or meaning</label><input id="search" type="search" placeholder="For example: indignation or trade"><label for="category">Show a category</label><select id="category"><option value="all">All categories</option>{options}</select><button id="clear" type="button">Clear filters</button></div><p id="count" role="status">{len(d)} entries</p>{sections}</section><section id="sources" class="reading-section"><h2>Sources and scope</h2><p>Both complete chapters were rescanned for this resource, including the opening and closing passages. This is a broad reading vocabulary, not a concordance of every word. It includes emotional language, moral and religious terms, power and law, commerce, seamanship, and older meanings. Definitions are written for these contexts.</p><p><a href="https://history.hanover.edu/texts/Equiano/equiano_ch5_a.html">Read Chapter V</a> · <a href="https://history.hanover.edu/texts/Equiano/equiano_ch6_a.html">Read Chapter VI</a> · <a href="equiano-vocabulary-assets/words.json">Complete vocabulary data and passage references</a></p><p>References labeled “transcription p.” follow the page markers in Hanover’s historical transcription, not the page numbers in your classroom edition. Use the chapter and quoted words to find a passage. Some terms occur in verse quoted within the narrative; their context is preserved. Original spelling is retained in quotations. Some source passages contain historical racial language and descriptions of violence.</p><p>Equiano’s descriptions of law, Providence, and human character are his claims and interpretations. The definitions do not independently verify every historical claim.</p></section><footer><a href="../courses/frst-110.html">FRST 110 course page</a><a href="#main">Back to top</a></footer></main><script id="word-data" type="application/json">{json.dumps(d,ensure_ascii=False).replace('</','<\\/')}</script></body></html>'''
